## Summary
A crash between the `git worktree add` call and the final `insertRegistryWorktree()` call inside `ManagedWorktreeService.create()` leaves an on-disk worktree directory and a local branch that no code path (`list`, `get`, `gc`, `reconcileOrphans`) can ever discover, register, or remove, and any later `create()` call with the same name then fails hard with "branch already exists".

## Environment
- Commit: 66e4f4869c0f48f0e3bd8d3d1443f313445191a4 (origin/main at time of filing)
- Surface: `src/agents/worktrees/service.ts`, `ManagedWorktreeService.create()` and `reconcileOrphans()`, used by the agent-task managed git worktree subsystem added in PR #100535

## Steps to reproduce
1. Call `ManagedWorktreeService.create({ repoRoot, name: "task-1" })` and let it run the real `git worktree add` (service.ts:386-411), then have the process die before the `insertRegistryWorktree()` call at service.ts:439 runs (in a real deployment this is an OOM kill, a forced restart, or any other crash landing in that window; the attached repro reproduces the same end state by making the registry insert throw right after the real `git worktree add` succeeds, so the on-disk git state is genuinely committed and nothing downstream of the git call cleans it up).
2. Observe that the worktree directory and the `openclaw/task-1` branch exist on disk and in `git worktree list`, but the SQLite `worktrees` registry has no row for that path.
3. Call `service.gc()` (which internally calls `reconcileOrphans()`).
4. Call `service.create({ repoRoot, name: "task-1" })` again.

## Expected
Either the orphaned directory/branch should be repaired into the registry or removed by `reconcileOrphans()`/`gc()`, and/or a later `create()` with the same name should succeed or fail with an actionable, self-recoverable error.

## Actual
`reconcileOrphans()` finds the orphan directory, resolves it as a real repository, sees that git still lists it as a live worktree, and explicitly skips it (service.ts:723-729) instead of repairing or deleting it. The directory is invisible to `list()`/`get()` forever (no registry row), survives every `gc()` pass forever (git still lists it, so the skip condition matches every time), and a subsequent `create()` call with the same name throws `branch already exists: openclaw/task-1` because the pre-existing-branch guard at service.ts:372-380 fires while the registry-row shortcut at service.ts:360-369 cannot help, since no row was ever inserted. There is no API path to see, restore, or delete the orphan; only a manual operator `git worktree remove` / `git branch -D` outside the managed API unblocks it.

## Root cause
`src/agents/worktrees/service.ts:386-439`:
```
let added = await runGit(repository.repoRoot, [
  "worktree",
  "add",
  worktreePath,
  "-b",
  branch,
  usedBase,
]);
...
if (added.code !== 0) {
  throw commandError("git worktree add", added);
}
try {
  await copyIncludedFiles(repository.sourceRoot, worktreePath);
  if (params.runSetupScript !== false) {
    await runSetupScript(repository.sourceRoot, worktreePath);
  }
} catch (error) {
  ...
  throw error;
}
const createdAt = this.now();
const record: ManagedWorktreeRecord = { ... };
insertRegistryWorktree(this.env, record);
return record;
```
`insertRegistryWorktree()` is the very last statement in `create()`, called after `git worktree add` has already created the directory and branch, and it sits outside the only `try`/`catch` in the function (which only wraps `copyIncludedFiles`/`runSetupScript` and only guards against failures in that narrower window). Any failure between the successful `git worktree add` and this final call, including a full process crash, leaves git-committed state with zero registry awareness and no cleanup path.

`src/agents/worktrees/service.ts:704-736` (`reconcileOrphans`), invoked from `gc()` at service.ts:677, is the only automated repair pass:
```
const repository = await resolveRepository(candidate).catch(() => undefined);
if (repository) {
  const listed = await listGitWorktrees(repository.repoRoot).catch(() => []);
  if (listed.some((entry) => path.resolve(entry.path) === path.resolve(candidate))) {
    continue;
  }
}
await fs.rm(candidate, { recursive: true, force: true });
deleted += 1;
```
For a directory git still lists as a live worktree, which is exactly the state a mid-create crash leaves, this loop explicitly `continue`s instead of registering or removing it, so the orphan is preserved forever by design.

## Sibling surfaces
This is distinct from the GC race in the same file being fixed separately; that one concerns concurrent `gc()`/removal interleaving on already-registered worktrees. This issue is about the create-time window before a registry row ever exists, which no GC or reconciliation logic addresses regardless of that fix. Not checked: whether the equivalent create paths in any other worktree-like registry-plus-external-resource pairing in the codebase have the same insert-last ordering; this report is scoped to `src/agents/worktrees/service.ts`.

## Real behavior proof
Behavior addressed: mid-create crash between git worktree add and the registry insert leaves a permanent orphan that also blocks recreation under the same name.
Real environment tested: real ManagedWorktreeService driving the real git binary and the real SQLite worktrees registry, at commit 66e4f4869c0f48f0e3bd8d3d1443f313445191a4, with only the final insertRegistryWorktree call replaced by a throw to stand in for a process crash at that exact point (the git worktree add call and every subsequent registry read/gc call are real and unmocked).
Exact steps or command run after this patch: a repro harness placed temporarily under src/agents/worktrees and run via node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/worktrees/zzz-repro-orphan.test.ts (never committed, removed immediately after the run); the harness drives service.create() with insertRegistryWorktree made to throw once, then reads back git worktree list, the SQLite registry, service.list(), service.gc(), and a second service.create() call with the same name.
Evidence after fix:
```
crash create() rejected: Error: simulated process crash before registry insert
orphan directory exists on disk after crash: true
git branch --list openclaw/task-1: "+ openclaw/task-1"
git worktree list --porcelain:
worktree /tmp/openclaw-worktree-orphan-xnlbLJ/repo
HEAD 519fb8a7006f71dc61e4437743ba5281a082aaa5
branch refs/heads/main

worktree /tmp/openclaw-worktree-orphan-xnlbLJ/openclaw-state/worktrees/b44f873693593f7b/task-1
HEAD 519fb8a7006f71dc61e4437743ba5281a082aaa5
branch refs/heads/openclaw/task-1
registry row for orphan path after crash: undefined
service.list() before gc: []
gc() removed=[] orphansDeleted=0
orphan directory exists on disk after gc(): true
registry row for orphan path after gc(): undefined
recreate create({ name: "task-1" }) rejected: Error: branch already exists: openclaw/task-1
```
Observed result after fix: after the simulated crash, git holds a real worktree directory and branch that the registry never recorded; service.list() omits it, the garbage collection pass leaves the orphan count at zero while the directory stays on disk, and a second create() call using the same name always fails with branch already exists, with no available API call to find, restore, or remove the orphan.
What was not tested: an actual OS-level process kill (SIGKILL) at that exact point rather than a simulated throw at the same call site; the remote-base fallback branch of create() (service.ts:394-408) under the same crash window; and any interaction with the separate GC race in this file that another change addresses.
